        <TopPick />

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The Short Answer
          </h2>

          <p>
            Vinegar has some effect on some molds, and that is about as far as the evidence goes. Household white vinegar is a dilute solution of acetic acid, usually around 5 percent, and acids do make life harder for many fungi. That is why the claim keeps circulating, and why so many cleaning guides repeat it. But "has some effect on some molds" is a long way from "kills mold", and the gap between those two phrases is where most people get let down.
          </p>

          <p>
            Three facts frame everything else on this page. First, the best-known laboratory comparison of household antifungal agents found that vinegar slowed one common indoor mold and did nothing measurable to another. Second, vinegar sold for cooking and cleaning is not registered with the EPA as a fungicide, so nobody has had to prove it kills mold at any concentration or contact time, and no bottle can legally promise that it does. Third, the EPA's own guidance on mold cleanup does not rely on any kill step at all: it says to scrub mold off hard surfaces with detergent and water and dry them completely.
          </p>

          <p>
            So, will vinegar kill mold? On a shower curtain liner, a window track or a tiled splashback with a light film of mildew, vinegar will help you clean it off, and cleaning it off is most of the job. On drywall, wood framing, a basement wall or anything larger than a small patch, vinegar is the wrong tool, and a registered fungicide such as RMR-141 is the better buy. On marble, limestone or travertine, vinegar can do more damage than the mold.
          </p>

          <p>
            Whatever you use, the mold will return if the moisture that fed it stays. That is the part people skip, and it is the part that decides whether you are cleaning the same corner again next month. The rest of this guide covers what the research actually shows, how vinegar compares with bleach and the other household options, how to use it properly if you decide to, and when it is worth spending money on a product with a real kill claim instead.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE SHORT VERSION</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Vinegar cleans light mildew. It is not a registered mold killer.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Use undiluted white vinegar for small spots on hard, non-porous surfaces if you like. For anything bigger, porous or recurring, use an EPA-registered fungicide with a stated contact time, and fix the humidity.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="research" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. What the Research Actually Shows
          </h2>

          <p>
            The study most often cited on this question is a 2015 study by Rogawansamy and colleagues at the University of Adelaide and Flinders University, published in the International Journal of Environmental Research and Public Health. The researchers compared five agents against two molds commonly found in indoor air, Aspergillus fumigatus and Penicillium chrysogenum: two commercial disinfectants, 70 percent ethanol, tea tree oil, and ordinary vinegar at 4.0 to 4.2 percent acetic acid. They chose vinegar partly because Australian mold guidelines had recommended vinegar in water for damp-wiping hard surfaces during cleanup.
          </p>

          <p>
            The result for vinegar was mixed. According to the paper, vinegar inhibited the growth of Penicillium chrysogenum and suppressed its spore production, but it showed no inhibitory effect on Aspergillus fumigatus. In other words, one of the two molds grew more slowly near the vinegar and the other grew as if it were not there. Tea tree oil was the most effective agent the authors tested, and the 70 percent ethanol had no inhibitory effect on either mold.
          </p>

          <p>
            You will sometimes read the opposite on the internet, that the study proved vinegar fails against Penicillium. That gets it backwards. The fair summary is that vinegar worked on one test species and not the other, which is exactly why it cannot be relied on: you do not know which mold is growing on your bathroom ceiling, and a cleaner that works on some species and not others is a gamble every time.
          </p>

          <p>
            It is also worth being clear about what kind of test this was. The researchers used a disc diffusion method, placing a small paper disc soaked in each agent on a dish of growing mold and measuring the clear zone around it. That measures inhibition in a lab dish, not whether every spore on a real wall is dead. The paper did not test vinegar on building materials, and it did not measure whether vinegar-treated mold regrew later. The authors only suggested that vinegar's lack of persistence on surfaces may limit its usefulness.
          </p>

          <p>
            Another figure you will see quoted is that vinegar kills 82 percent of mold species. We could not trace that number to a published study, so we have left it out of our advice. If a guide gives you a precise percentage for vinegar without naming the research behind it, treat the number with the same suspicion you would give a bottle claiming to kill mold without an EPA registration.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Agent tested (2015 study)</th>
