        <div id="no-kill-claim" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Why Vinegar Carries No Kill Claim
          </h2>

          <p>
            In the United States, any product that claims to kill a living organism, whether bacteria, viruses or fungi, is a pesticide in the legal sense and must be registered with the EPA before it can make that claim. Registration means the maker has submitted efficacy data, and the approved label then spells out which organisms it kills, on which surfaces, at what dilution and for how long the surface has to stay wet. That number, printed as EPA Reg. No. on the label, is the difference between a cleaner and a fungicide.
          </p>

          <p>
            Grocery-store vinegar has none of that. The EPA itself has said it does not review the effectiveness of common household ingredients such as vinegar or rubbing alcohol, so it cannot verify how well they work against germs. There is no approved contact time for vinegar on mold, no list of species it controls, and no surface directions. When a blog tells you to leave vinegar on for an hour, that figure is household advice, not a label requirement backed by testing.
          </p>

          <p>
            Compare that with RMR-141, the fungicide we recommend first in our <a href="/best-mold-remover/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">guide to the best mold removers</a>. Its EPA registration number is 61178-2-93443, its active ingredients are two quaternary ammonium compounds at 0.07 percent each, and the label directions for mold are specific: saturate the surface and keep it wet for 10 minutes, then wipe or let it air dry. The maker says it kills more than 140 microorganisms. Those claims can be checked against an approved label, which is the whole point of registration.
          </p>

          <p>
            None of this means vinegar does nothing. Acetic acid is genuinely hostile to many microbes, and plenty of people keep a bathroom mildew-free with a vinegar spray bottle. It means that if you need to be confident mold is dead, for example on wood framing you are about to close up behind drywall, vinegar gives you no basis for that confidence. A registered product does, provided you follow its contact time.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="vinegar-vs-bleach" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Vinegar vs Bleach for Mold
          </h2>

          <p>
            Vinegar vs bleach for mold is the comparison most people are really making, because those are the two bottles under the sink. They behave very differently. Bleach is sodium hypochlorite, a strong oxidiser. On hard, non-porous surfaces such as glazed tile and grout it kills surface mold and strips the dark staining quickly, which is why bleach sprays like Clorox Plus Tilex are a reasonable choice for black spots in a shower. Clorox says Tilex kills 99.9 percent of mold and mildew.
          </p>

          <p>
            Bleach's problem is porous materials. On drywall, wood or concrete it whitens the stain so the surface looks clean while doing little about growth below the surface, and the water it carries adds moisture. The EPA does not recommend biocides like chlorine bleach as a routine part of mold cleanup. Our companion article on whether <a href="/does-bleach-kill-mold/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">bleach kills mold</a> goes through where it works and where it misleads you.
          </p>

          <p>
            Vinegar is milder on the nose and on most surfaces, it does not whiten fabric, and it is cheap enough to use generously. It is weaker as a stain remover, so dead mold often leaves a mark behind, and it has no registered kill claim to rely on. On the porous-material question it is no better than bleach: it cannot reach growth inside drywall paper or deep in soft wood any more reliably, and soaking a porous surface with any liquid can make things worse if you do not dry it.
          </p>

          <p>
            The one rule that matters more than which is better: never mix vinegar and bleach, and never use them one after the other on the same surface without rinsing between. Bleach mixed with acids, including vinegar, releases chlorine gas. Clorox warns against mixing Tilex with acids, toilet bowl cleaners or rust removers for the same reason. Pick one product, use it, rinse thoroughly, and ventilate the room.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">SAFETY RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Never combine vinegar and bleach.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Acid plus sodium hypochlorite releases chlorine gas. Do not mix them in a bottle, and do not spray one onto a surface still wet with the other. Rinse and let the area dry between products, and keep the room ventilated.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="peroxide-alcohol-baking-soda" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Hydrogen Peroxide, Alcohol and Baking Soda
          </h2>

          <p>
            Once people learn vinegar is unreliable, the next questions are usually about the other household staples. The honest answer for all three is the same as for vinegar: none of the versions in your medicine cabinet or pantry carries an EPA-registered kill claim for mold on household surfaces, so there is no approved contact time to follow and no guarantee. Here is what can be said about each.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Will hydrogen peroxide kill mold?</h3>

          <p>
            Hydrogen peroxide is a genuine antimicrobial, and some EPA-registered disinfectants and fungicides use it as their active ingredient, with mold or mildew on their approved labels. That is a meaningful difference from vinegar. But the brown bottle of 3 percent peroxide from the pharmacy is sold as a first-aid antiseptic, not as a registered mold product, so it comes with no mold directions or contact time. It can also lighten some fabrics and finishes. If you want peroxide chemistry, buy a registered product and read its label. Never mix peroxide and vinegar in the same container: the combination forms a corrosive acid.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Does alcohol kill mold?</h3>

          <p>
            The evidence here is weaker than most people expect. In the 2015 Rogawansamy study described above, 70 percent ethanol had no inhibitory effect on either of the two molds tested, which is a worse result than vinegar managed. Most rubbing alcohol in the US is isopropyl rather than ethanol, and that study did not test it, so we will not claim it fails. What we can say is that the EPA does not review household rubbing alcohol for effectiveness, it evaporates quickly, and it is flammable, which makes it a poor choice for soaking a wall.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Does baking soda kill mold?</h3>

          <p>
            Baking soda is a mild alkaline powder, and its real value in mold cleanup is as a gentle abrasive and deodoriser. A paste of baking soda and water helps scrub a stain out of grout without scratching tile, and it will not etch stone the way vinegar can. It is not a registered fungicide and we found no reliable evidence that it kills mold on household surfaces. Use it as the scrubbing step, then rinse, and do not mix it with vinegar in a bottle; the acid and the alkali neutralise each other, leaving mostly water and a weak salt solution that does neither job well.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
