        <div id="how-to-use" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. How to Use Vinegar on Mold Properly
          </h2>

          <p>
            If your problem is a light film of mildew on a hard surface, vinegar is a cheap and reasonable cleaner, and it is worth using properly rather than in a quick spray-and-wipe. Start by checking the surface. Glazed ceramic tile, glass, porcelain, fiberglass tubs, plastic shower liners, vinyl window frames and painted metal are fine. Natural stone that contains calcium, which means marble, limestone and travertine, is not, because acid reacts with the stone and leaves a dull etch mark that cleaning will not remove. Grout is cement-based and can also be worn by repeated acid cleaning.
          </p>

          <p>
            Use plain distilled white vinegar, undiluted, straight from the bottle into a clean spray bottle. Diluting it with water, as some guides suggest, only weakens an already mild acid. Do not add bleach, ammonia-based cleaners or hydrogen peroxide. Put on gloves, open a window or run the bathroom fan, and if you are dealing with more than a few small spots, wear an N-95 respirator and goggles, which is the EPA's minimum kit for a small mold cleanup.
          </p>

          <p>
            Spray until the area is visibly wet and leave it. Cleaning guides commonly suggest about an hour of contact before scrubbing; that figure comes from household advice rather than any approved label, so treat it as a sensible minimum rather than a guarantee. Re-spray if the surface starts to dry, especially on vertical tile where the vinegar runs off. Then scrub with a stiff brush or a baking soda paste, wipe the residue away with clean water, and dry the surface completely with a towel or by running the fan.
          </p>

          <p>
            Expect the staining to fade but not always disappear. Mold pigment can remain after the growth itself is gone, especially in grout lines and caulk. If dark marks persist on tile or grout and the surface can take bleach, a dedicated stain remover is the next step, used on its own after the vinegar has been rinsed off and the surface has dried. Silicone caulk that has gone black inside is usually not worth cleaning at all; cutting it out and recaulking is faster and lasts longer.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, look at why it grew. Mildew on a shower ceiling usually means the room stays humid too long after bathing. Mildew on a window track means condensation. Vinegar removes the symptom; running the exhaust fan for 20 minutes after showers, or lowering indoor humidity, removes the cause. If the same spot is back within a few weeks, that is your signal that cleaning alone will not win.
          </p>

