        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Vinegar is a decent cleaner for light mildew on tile, glass and other hard, non-porous surfaces, and a poor bet as a mold killer. The research shows it slowing one common mold and not another, and it carries no EPA registration, so there is no tested contact time behind any claim. Keep it away from marble and limestone, and never let it meet bleach.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            When you need mold actually dead, on wood, drywall or anything that keeps coming back, use a registered fungicide such as RMR-141 and keep it wet for its full 10 minutes. Then hold indoor humidity between 30 and 50 percent, because a dry room is the only thing that stops mold returning.
          </p>
